## The idea

The list is sorted and the intervals do not overlap, so it falls into **three runs**: intervals that end before the new one starts, intervals that overlap it, and intervals that start after it ends. Copy the first run, **fold** the middle run into the new interval, copy the last run.

## Step by step

1. Copy intervals whose end < new start.
2. While the next interval's start ≤ new end: widen the new interval to cover it.
3. Add the (widened) new interval.
4. Copy everything left.

## Worked example

**Input:** intervals = [1,2] [3,5] [6,7] [8,10] [12,16], add [4,8]

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| run 1 | [1,2] ends before 4 → copy |
| run 2 | [3,5] overlaps → [3,8]; [6,7] → [3,8]; [8,10] touches → [3,10] |
| add | [3,10] |
| run 3 | [12,16] → copy |

**Result:** [1,2] [3,10] [12,16]

## Cost

O(n) time — one pass. O(n) for the output.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
// LeetCode 57 — insert one interval into a sorted, non-overlapping list, merging where needed.
public static class IntervalInsert {
    public static int[][] Insert(int[][] intervals, int[] add) {
        var result = new List<int[]>();
        int n = intervals == null ? 0 : intervals.Length;
        int i = 0, start = add[0], end = add[1];

        // 1. Intervals that end before the new one starts are untouched.
        while (i < n && intervals[i][1] < start) { result.Add(intervals[i]); i++; }

        // 2. Intervals that overlap (or touch) the new one are absorbed into it.
        while (i < n && intervals[i][0] <= end) {
            start = Math.Min(start, intervals[i][0]);
            end = Math.Max(end, intervals[i][1]);
            i++;
        }
        result.Add(new[] { start, end });

        // 3. Everything left starts after the new one ends.
        while (i < n) { result.Add(intervals[i]); i++; }
        return result.ToArray();
    }
}
```

## Likely follow-up questions

**Q: Touching intervals — merge?**

For closed intervals, yes. Ask; half-open intervals would not.

**Q: Unsorted input?**

Then sort first, and say it now costs O(n log n).

**Q: Complexity?**

O(n) time, O(n) for the output.

**Q: Faster than O(n)?**

Binary-search where the overlap begins and ends — but building the output is still O(n) with an array.

**Q: Many threads insert into a shared calendar.**

One lock around the whole insert, or copy-on-write: build the new list, swap a reference.

**Q: Millions of intervals, frequent inserts.**

A balanced tree keyed by start (SortedDictionary / SortedSet) makes each insert O(log n + merged) — InterviewDB Q9 is this structure.

**Q: How would you test it?**

Brute force on a bitmap of small coordinates over random inputs.
